## Context

The author asked (2026-09-30) to explore TypeSafe's Jev model for fast,
structured classification tasks. On OpenRouter on 2026-09-30 the listing
shows `typesafe/jev-router` ("picks the best model and reasoning effort for
each request ... runs on Jev, TypeSafe's first System One model"), context
1M tokens, pricing reported as variable (the API returns no fixed price) and
no supported-parameter list; the model page links a `jev-latest` that the
public models list does not return. Not established: whether Jev itself is
callable directly, its price, structured-output support, data retention.

Candidate uses in the specification: the classification steps of extraction
(line classification, dispositions) and collection triage, where the two
readers answer from a closed list; the specification selects readers and the
arbiter on OpenRouter against held-out reference answers before use
(`docs/jetp-operation.md`, selection and calibration; `docs/jetp-extraction.md`,
reading and calibration). A router that changes the underlying model per
request conflicts with reproducible, calibrated readers unless the chosen
model is recorded per call.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Callable routes, price, structured-output (JSON schema) support and data-handling terms of Jev and Jev Router established and recorded
- [ ] Jev scored on the held-out reference answers for one classification task, beside the current candidates, with cost and latency
- [ ] A recommendation recorded in the model-selection record: candidate reader, arbiter, or not used, with the router's reproducibility question answered
